ext","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"pol"}}],"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xiv, 251 p. ; 19 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"In the 1950's Eastern Europe was in the grip of Stalinism; still, many Western intellectuals mistakenly placed their hopes in the new world in the East. That time is now past, yet totalitarian thinking, whether of the left or the right, exerts -- and will continue to exert -- a powerful attraction on the minds of men. In \"The Captive Mind\", Czeslaw Milosz takes us into the world inhabited by the people of Communist Poland, Czechoslovakia, Rumania and Hungary, and reveals in fascinating and frightening detail what happens to the human mind under totalitarianism: the often compelling reasons that lead men to embrace it and why -- given the spiritual price of living on terms established by an omnipotent state -- there is the urgent need to find political solutions elsewhere. -- From publisher's description."},"tableOfContents":"The pill of Murti-Bing -- Looking to the West -- Ketman -- Alpha, the moralist -- Beta, the disappointed lover -- Gamma, the slave of history -- Delta, the troubdour -- Man, this enemy -- The lesson of the Baltics.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"by Czeslaw Milosz ; translated from the Polish by Jane Zielonko."},"Translation of: Zniewolony umysł."],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-pl---"}},{"temporal":"Geschichte 1930-1956"},{"@authority":"fast","temporal":"1945-1989"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Communism","geographic":"Poland"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Poland","topic":"Intellectual life","temporal":"1945-1989"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"DK4437 .M5413 1981b"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"000108142"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Onlin